export function methodView(){return `<h1>Method & limits</h1><p><button data-page="bibliography">Bibliography & sources</button></p><div class="notice">Release validation: 91 automated tests passed, 0 failed (2026-11-06, Node 22.18.0), including Docker/local JSON parity, legacy migration, event integrity and statistical acceptance cases. These tests do not certify a real factory.</div><section class="panel"><h2>Canonical model and execution</h2><p>One JSON model (schema 2) supplies all diagrams and discrete-event simulation. Flow, resource, staffing and material relations execute; other UML/SysML relations are descriptive. Sources emit at t=0 and include the horizon boundary. Finite buffers exclude processing channels; source backlog is included in final WIP. Service order follows operation order and can starve another line. Resources are released before output blocking. No optimal scheduling, 3D, fork/join, product-specific recipes or full UAF/TOGAF execution.</p><h2>Operation phases</h2><p>Legacy schema 1 preserves fraction-based durations and the old random sequence. Native phase schema 2 provides load, automatic, unload, walk/transfer, inspect and wait, with independent durations in seconds, constant/bounded-uniform distributions and source notes (MEASURED/SPEC/ASSUMPTION). Technical operation resources remain reserved across the sequence; temporary phase resources are acquired atomically and released at phase boundaries. Direct and inherited demands add, with duplicate warnings.</p><p>A sequence-held resource cannot also be temporary anywhere in the model: this restrictive partition prevents circular resource acquisition, not routing/buffer deadlocks. Native automatic phases cannot reserve people. Native MTBF/MTTR failures apply only in automatic phases; repair is separate and holds active resources. Legacy operations hold the operator throughout their cycle and repair. Setup is per part; ON/OFF calendars gate new starts without interrupting ongoing work.</p></section><section class="panel"><h2>Inspectable event ledger and quality</h2><p>Each replication retains unit IDs, arrivals/dispositions, phase events, resource ownership, material movements, blocking, starvation, failures/repairs, calendars, inspection outcomes, scrap, rework, retest and downtime. Missing incident codes use UNSPECIFIED and generate counted data-quality warnings. Configure code/name/class/severity/origin and assignments under Results / Quality codes. Codes describe outcomes without changing probabilities. Inspection results occur at operation completion, not independently at each internal inspect phase.</p><p>Rework is explicitly marked or inferred from a return to an earlier station. Normal forward travel after that return is not another incident. Materials are consumed again on reprocessing. Passing inspection has no defect code. Complete event logs are separate from the older 2000-interval trace. Filters, timeline, CSV and JSON export are available for each replication.</p><p>Replay checks ownership/capacity, unit conservation, material balance, state coverage, code totals and numeric KPI agreement. State totals are capacity-normalized seconds summing to the horizon. Active work takes priority over OFF; free pool capacity is idle/off-shift, not inferred starved. Open phases/repairs remain open at the horizon and their units are WIP. No cryptographic tamper proofing is provided.</p></section><section class="panel"><h2>Statistics and observation window</h2><p>Every scalar KPI and resource/operation utilization uses all available replications: mean, sample standard deviation, min/max and a configurable two-sided Student-t interval (confidence 0.8 to 0.999). One replication has no interval. Undefined quantities are excluded with N valid/all shown; cost per good is undefined for zero-good runs. This exclusion can bias that estimate. Ratio intervals are not clipped to [0,1]. Intervals concern the estimated mean, not all future outcomes or input/model uncertainty.</p><p>Lead time uses completed units and retains their full age from arrival. Unfinished final WIP is shown alongside it. Warm-up defaults to 0 and only clips the observation window; it does not reset dynamics. A warning is shown when warm-up excludes the start of an empty shift. Costs/occupancy are clipped to the same window; final WIP remains the full horizon inventory.</p><p>FPY pools first completed attempts across stations. Observed end-to-end RTY is good terminal units with no rework divided by all terminal units; it is not the product of yields for an assumed fixed route. Rework load is busy seconds on repeat station visits. Scrap rate is scrap divided by terminal good plus scrap; unfinished units are excluded. Detailed first-replication tables are explicitly labelled; aggregate tables and headline means use every replication.</p><h2>Scenarios and random streams</h2><p>Matching seeds support paired replication-difference intervals; otherwise the comparison uses a Welch independent-sample approximation. Same seed does not guarantee identical event-level random pairs between different models. Optional CRN separates duration, quality and routing by stable operation ID, and failure/repair streams by the first sorted assigned machine/robot/hardware ID (or operation ID without one). Changed IDs, scheduling or draw counts can reduce or reverse variance reduction. This stream policy does not introduce a shared physical machine-failure model. No automatic optimization recommendation is made.</p></section><section class="panel"><h2>Experiments and persistence</h2><p>Experiments automatically save the model snapshot, settings, explicit seeds, engine/analysis versions, results and timestamp in this browser's IndexedDB, including when Docker computes the run. The results page lists experiments and supports opening, exporting and rerunning their snapshots. Deterministic comparison excludes wall-clock completion metadata; historical results are not silently updated. Exact replay requires matching engine/analysis versions and runtime behavior. Browser storage can be denied, cleared or quota-limited; errors are shown and JSON export is recommended. This is not a shared server experiment archive. The comparison baseline remains session-local.</p><h2>Budgets and limitations</h2><p>Maximum 50 replications, 500 elements, 2000 relations and 50 diagrams. Experiments have a combined 200000-event/32 MiB serialized-audit budget and a 45-second between-replication time guard. Docker additionally limits workers to 256 MiB and 60 seconds. Excessive workloads abort rather than silently truncate logs. Not every model can run 50 replications within those combined budgets.</p><p>Benchmark (Node 22.18.0 on this Windows host, 2026-11-06): 50 replications of one part through a 60 s operation, uniform variation +/-20%, 180 s horizon: about 75 ms, 9.9 MiB heap at completion and 2.21 MiB serialized output. This justifies 50 for small experiments only; it is not a large-factory scalability benchmark.</p><p>Energy includes processing only. Operation cost includes processing, repair and phase waits; resource cost follows reserved time, so avoid double accounting. No authentication/RBAC, collaborative live editing, external replenishment, sequence-dependent setup, calibrated repair crews or production deployment qualification is implied. Provenance is not mandatory for every value in older models.</p><h2>Reference exercise</h2><p>Source 60 s, preparation 30 s, assembly 45 s, test 90 s, one shared preparation/assembly operator and one test bench, buffers 10: 319 good units in 8 h versus a 40/h bound. With test at 60 s: 379 versus a 48/h operator bound; unfinished processing accounts for the finite-horizon difference. These are synthetic test assumptions, not manufacturer process data.</p></section>`;}
